casebook conflicted-fixture v1
source "adversarial fixture for the Conflicted outcome (not a real asset)" dated 2026-09-22 by "quorum tests"
note "This casebook is DELIBERATELY BROKEN and cbc must refuse it. Do not fix it."
note ""
note "Why it exists: `Conflicted` -- more than one action selected at once -- is tested at the arbiter level by"
note "handing it two unattacked actions directly. That proves the arbiter classifies it. It does not prove that a"
note "casebook an engineer could plausibly write can reach it, nor that the compiler catches it and says something"
note "useful. Both real casebooks report Conflicted in 0 states, so neither can test this."
note ""
note "The mistake is realistic and small: two actions, each with a sensible condition, each ordered against the idle"
note "action, and NOTHING ordering them against each other. When both conditions hold the framework has no grounds"
note "to choose. An author who adds a third action to a working casebook and extends the prefer chain by one line"
note "makes exactly this mistake."
note ""
note "It is the dangerous direction, too: 'open the valve' and 'close the valve' can both be In, and picking either"
note "is a guess dressed as a decision. The arbiter returns the fallback instead, and the compiler refuses to emit."

observe x "condition x asserted"
observe y "condition y asserted"

claim cx "x means the valve should open"  needs x
claim cy "y means the valve should close" needs y

action idle_act    idle
action open_valve  when cx
action close_valve safe when cy

# The defect: open_valve and close_valve are each ordered above idle_act, and not against each other.
prefer open_valve over idle_act
prefer close_valve over idle_act
